|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_project_i_and_j_and_k_

    A unit vector 

    $$
    !! (x_2, y_2, z_2) \in \rr^3
    (x_1, y_1, z_1) \in \rr^3
    $$

    [meaning: $x_1^2 + y_1^2 + z_1^2 = 1$] is projected onto a ruler
    whose ‘$0$’ is at $(0, 0, 0)$ and whose ‘$1$’ is at
    $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$, another point on the unit sphere:

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/e5_ruler_sphere_1_v2.svg

    !! (By “is projected onto the ruler” we mean that we drop a
    !! plumb line from the tip of the vector onto the ruler, meeting
    !! the edge of the ruler at $90°$.)
    Find the value of the projection—the place we land on the
    ruler, at $90^\circ$—as a
    function of $x_1$, $y_1$, $z_1$, $x_2$, $y_2$, $z_2$.

    |> Solution

        Putting

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        \vi = (1,\, 0,\, 0),         \\
        \vj = (0,\, 1,\, 0),\up{1.2} \\
        \vk = (0,\, 0,\, 1)\,\up{1.2}
        \end{gathered}
        $$
        
        !! —the so-called “standard basis”—
        we have

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        x_1\cdot\vi = (x_1, \,0,   \,0),         \\
        y_1\cdot\vj = (0,   \,y_1, \,0)\up{1.2}, \\
        z_1\cdot\vk = (0,   \,0,   \,z_1)\up{1.2}\, 
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        giving

        $$
        (x_1, y_1, z_1) = x_1\!\cdot\vi + y_1\!\cdot\vj + z_1\!\cdot\vk
        $$

        because vectors are added component-wise. 
        This sum can be illustrated:
        !! can be effected by placing the vectors head-to-tail, as
        !! illustrated here:

        !! where
        !! $$
        !! x_1\!\cdot\vi,\qquad y_1\!\cdot\vj,\qquad z_1\!\cdot\vk
        !! $$
        !! are also the so-called
        !! __components__
        !! of $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ along the $x$, $y$ and $z$ axes:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_ruler_sphere_decomp.svg

        !! where $\vi = (1, 0, 0)$,
        !! $\vj = (0, 1, 0)$,
        !! $\vk = (0, 1, 0)$, gives this figure:
        !! and note that the sum-of-the-projections is the sum-of-the-projections,
        !! as in this figure:

        !! As per Note 1 of Exercise 11,
        !! we may speak of
        !! __the projection__
        !! (emphasis on “the”, uniqueness) of a vector onto the
        !! ruler while translating the vector to any point in space. The
        !! projection, defined as the difference in coordinates between
        !! the projections of the head and tail of the 
        !! [representation of the]
        !! vector
        !! individually, is invariant.
        !! For example, here is a projection of $y_1\!\cdot\vj$

        As discussed in Note 1 of Exercise 11 we can
        translate vectors to anywhere in space while
        preserving their 
        projection onto the ruler. 
        !! “shadow”—the difference of
        !! coordinates, also what we simply call the _projection_ of
        !! the vector—on
        !! the ruler. 
        !! Keeping the same positioning in space of the
        !! vectors as above, one can see that the projection of
        !! $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ is the sum of the separate projections 
        !! of $x_1\vi$, $y_1\rt{0.1}\vj$,  $z_1\vk$:
        In particular, keeping the same positioning in space of the
        vectors as above shows that the projection of
        $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ is the sum of the three separate projections 
        of $x_1\vi$, $y_1\rt{0.1}\vj$,  $z_1\vk$:

        !! Moreover, the projection-of-the-sum is the
        !! sum-of-the-projections:

        !! Note that the projection-of-the-sum is the
        !! sum-of-the-projections:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_ruler_sphere_decomp3.svg

        !! (Or more precisely, we can translate vectors to
        !! anywhere in space without affecting their 
        !! |> del
        !!     “shadow”
        !! \ projection
        !! on the ruler, and if we translate them to the positions
        !! shown above then it becomes particularly obvious that the
        !! sum of the three 
        !! |> del
        !!     shadows
        !! \ projections is the projection of the original vector—this
        !! is the same topic of discussion as Note 1 of Exercise 14.)

        !! It therefore suffices to find the projections of
        !! $x_1\! \cdot\vi$, $y_1\! \cdot\vj$, and $z_1\! \cdot\vk$


        !! (Basically, as you move through space, you
        !! can check however often to look back at your “progress”
        !! on the ruler, and the sum the progresses over
        !! individual steps adds up to the total start-to-end progress.)

        !! (Basically, as you move through space, you
        !! can check however often to look back at your “progress”
        !! on the ruler, and the sum the progresses over
        !! individual steps adds up to the total start-to-end progress—and 
        !! we should clarify that the same vector can be projected
        !! onto the ruler from any point in
        !! space, the projection, as “progress” or difference in coordinates,
        !! is the same:)

        !! Note that we are allowed to speak of “the projection
        !! of a vector” while drawing the vector at any point in
        !! space because the difference between the projection of the
        !! head and tail of the (translated) vector remains constant,
        !! no matter where we drag the vector. E.g., here, the vector
        !! $z_1\!\cdot \vk$ is projected from three different places in
        !! space, but the projections, interpreted as a difference of
        !! ruler measurements from “start” to “end”, are the same:)


        !! ...though a crucial distinction,
        !! initially swept under the rug in Exercise 10,
        !! is that we can
        !! translate vectors to anywhere in space without
        !! affecting the length of their shadow (a.k.a., their
        !! projection) on the ruler—otherwise, we couldn't
        !! speak e.g. of “the projection of $z_1\!\cdot \vk$”
        !! to begin with
        !! as if it was some unique thing, while translating the
        !! vector anywhere in space. (But it _is_ a unique thing,
        !! and we _can_ translate the vector to anywhere in
        !! space before taking its projection!)


        !! Therefore, it suffices to find the projections of
        !! $x_1\vi$, $y_1\rt{0.1}\vj$, and $z_1\vk$
        !! separately in order to find the projection of $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$.

        !! Focusing on the case of $z_1\vk$, it
        !! suffices to find the projection of $\vk$ itself to
        !! find the projection of $z_1\vk$, as the projection
        !! of $z_1\vk$ is the projection of $\vk$ scaled by a
        !! factor $z_1$ (just like $z_1\vk$ is $\vk$ scaled by
        !! a factor $z_1$):

        !! Moreover, we can find the projections of
        !! $x_1\vi$, $\rt{0.1}y_1\rt{0.1}\vj\rt{0.1}$ and $z_1\vk\rt{0.1}$ if we can
        !! find the projections of $\vi$, $\rt{0.1}\vj\rt{0.1}$ and $\rt{0.1}\vk\rt{0.1}$ since
        !! the projection of $x_1\vi\rt{0.1}$ will just be the projection
        !! of $\vi$ multiplied by $x_1$, etc.
        !! !! The idea is illustrated 
        !! This is illustrated here with the
        !! case of $\vk$ and $z_1\vk\rt{0.1}$:
        !! !! that we have now translated back to the origin:

        !! But the projection of $z_1\vk$ is $z_1$ times
        !! the projection of $\vk$:
        !! because $z_1\vk$ is the vector $\vk$
        !! scaled by a factor $z_1$,
        !! their projections are likewise related):

        However, the projection of $z_1\vk$ is just 
        $z_1$ times the projection of $\vk$, as illustrated
        below, so it suffices to find the projection of $\vk$
        to find the projection of $z_1\vk$:
        !! $$
        !! z_1\vk
        !! $$

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_ruler_sphere_z_scale.svg

        The projection of $\vk$ 
        is the same as the projection of $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$ onto
        a ruler with ‘0’ at the origin and ‘1’ at $\vk$,
        because when one unit vector is projected onto another
        the situation is symmetric, and we can always switch 
        the role
        of which vector is the ruler, and which is being
        projected (cf. Solution 3 of Exercise 0):

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_ruler_sphere_z_switched_combined.svg

        But the 
        projection 
        of $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$ onto $\vk$
        equals simply

        $$
        z_2
        $$

        by the fact that 
        we are projecting onto the $z$ axis,
        so the projection of $\vk$ onto $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$
        is

        $$
        z_2
        $$

        as well,
        and the projection of $z_1\vk$ onto $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$ is

        $$
        z_1z_2
        $$

        by scaling.
        
        Likewise, we find that

        $$
        y_1y_2
        $$

        is the projection of $y_1\rt{0.1}\vj$ onto $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$,
        and that

        $$
        x_1x_2
        $$

        is the projection of $x_1\vi$ onto $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$, so

        |> Boxed
            $$
            x_1x_2 + y_1y_2 + z_1z_2
            $$
        
        is the projection of $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ onto $(x_2, y_2, z_2)$,
        summing the projections of $x_1\vi$, $y_1\vj$, and $z_1\vk$
        together.